feat(mcp): add observe_session (#207)

The eighth and last tool of IP §4's surface. It is the reference shim's
SessionStart and SessionEnd in one call, plus the marker bookkeeping that lived
under the harness's own runs directory and now lives inside the MCP, keyed on
the harness's session id.

It composes the other three rather than re-deriving anything: describe_workspace
resolves the workspace, register_agent mints the run, retire_agent ends it — the
SHIPPED tools, installed through their own Configure functions and called in
process, which is SignCommitThroughGetCredential's move for the same reason.
One derivation of what a workspace is, one definition of what a run is, one gate
that refuses a retired one. So this file holds no SPIRE client, no ledger and no
idempotency store; its only dependency is the volume the markers live on.

A STOP NEVER BLOCKS, and that decided the whole shape. A refused stop was
answered by the harness with nine repeated invocations, and an MCP tool's
equivalent of exit 2 is an error result — so every failure on the stop path
comes back as a successful reply carrying the class and the message in `detail`:
the ledger gone, SPIRE refusing the deletion, the marker unreadable, retire_agent
unwired. A start may refuse, and must, because IP §6.1 admits no attributed work
without an identity; the classes it returns are register_agent's and
describe_workspace's own, since IP §4's vocabulary is closed (doc 08 §3).

THE MARKER OUTLIVES THE RETIREMENT. The shim deleted it on the way into a stop
and measured the cost: a failed retirement left the run unretired with no record
to retry from, and three runs were still Active seventeen hours later. Keeping
it afterwards too, with the instant written in, buys a second thing: a run id is
a pure function of (agent_type, task, key), so a start for a session id already
stopped would derive the SAME run id and register_agent would replay a retired
run's reply — a dead identity handed back as a live one. A retired marker is
what lets a start report the terminal state instead. The limit is stated in the
file rather than left to be found: a run retired by a direct retire_agent call
leaves a marker that still reads live, and that is #213's class of problem, not
closed here.

The marker's file name is a digest of the session id, not the id. A caller's
string must never name a path, and two ids differing only in case must not
collide into one marker — which they would on the case-insensitive filesystem an
operator's machine usually has, giving two sessions one run. The session id
itself is deliberately NOT held to doc 02 §5's identifier grammar: it is a
foreign harness's string, and E11 exists so a second harness can participate.

No new error class and no schema change. An unwritable marker volume reports
LEDGER_UNAVAILABLE, retryable, which is observe_tool_call's reading for its own
body volume; the recorded link from a session to its run is the idempotency key
register_agent already writes, because doc 02 §3 has no member for a session id
and E11 is additive by construction.

Driven by MCP-050..MCP-054, written first and observed failing twice: once on
the undefined names, then against a stub that answered nothing, so every row
failed for what doc 07 says rather than for a missing symbol. 100% of statements
on internal/mcp, every error return driven — the unwritable volume by a real
filesystem refusal rather than a stub, and by one that is uid-independent, so
the case cannot quietly pass under root.

RM-131's three ratchets fire here and are cleared with it: the entry point's
surface assertion is renamed and its outstanding list is empty, the start-up
report is asserted silent rather than warning, and test/failure's
outstandingTools is empty for the first time since #210.

BOUND IS NOT CONFIGURED. Nothing wires this tool in the entry point, so it
answers INVARIANT_VIOLATION to every input until #211 does — the same state the
other two E11 tools are in.

